<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Croatia</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Zagreb</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A republic on the Adriatic that declared independence from Yugoslavia in 1991, won its War of Independence by 1995, and joined the EU in 2013.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Croatia declared independence on 25 June 1991 from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia</a>, and the declaration took full effect on 8 October 1991 after a three-month moratorium. The first multi-party elections of 1990 had been won by Franjo Tuđman. The Yugoslav People's Army and Serb paramilitary groups attacked, and by the end of 1991 Croatia controlled about two-thirds of its territory.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Croatia was recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> on 15 January 1992, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. The war ended effectively in August 1995, with most sources putting war deaths at around 20,000. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> were expelled or displaced in the occupied areas, and about 200,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> fled the self-proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-serbian-krajina">Republic of Serbian Krajina</a> after the Croatian victory. The remaining occupied areas returned to Croatia under the Erdut Agreement of November 1995, completed in January 1998. Constitutional amendments of 2000 and 2001 made parliament unicameral and limited presidential powers.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Croatia joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in April 2009 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in July 2013 as its 28th member. It joined the eurozone and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-area">Schengen Area</a> in 2023. Andrej Plenković has been prime minister since 19 October 2016, and Zoran Milanović was elected president in the election of 12 January 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/croatia.jpg" alt="Croatia" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Nightstallion, Elephantus, Neoneo13, Denelson83, Rainman, R-41, Minestrone, Lupo, Zscout370, Ma Ga (based on Decision of the Parliament ) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Croatia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zagreb</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Franjo Tuđman, Ivo Josipović, Andrej Plenković, Zoran Milanović</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic, Eastern Orthodox, Muslim</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Croatian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Croatia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=croatia" aria-label="Croatia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5058 563 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Croatia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5378 819L5488 850"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5378 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5378 819h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zagreb. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=croatia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
